123 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 378 0 R
/T ( INTRODUCTION )
/E ( INTRODUCTION )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
124 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (What would happen if the electricity went out suddenly in Kuwait, especially given the recent challenges? This has caused widespread fear, leading many to consider buying generators. In contrast, many developed neighboring countries were better prepared for such situations. Our investigation revealed that they have heavily relied on renewable energy, particularly solar panels. This inspired the idea for our project, with a primary focus on the Khaldiya district.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
125 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (Kuwait needs to rely on renewable energy sources because it has depended heavily on fossil fuels, which have negatively impacted the environment. Unlike fossil fuels, renewable energy sources are sustainable and naturally replenished, such as sunlight and wind. Kuwait relies primarily on oil and natural gas for electricity generation. Due to the extremely hot weather, especially in the summer, residents use air conditioning extensively, creating a significant demand for electricity. Furthermore, factories in Kuwait require substantial amounts of electricity, and unfortunately, they rely on fossil fuels to operate. Therefore, Kuwait must shift toward cleaner energy sources such as solar energy, which is cleaner to use, in order to reduce pollution and meet future energy needs.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
126 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (Solar energy is very suitable for Kuwait because it has high solar radiation throughout the year, which means that using solar panels is the best way to efficiently produce a large amount of electricity. Solar energy is also clean and helps reduce pollution and dependence on gas and oil. This project focuses on designing a photovoltaic \(PV\) solar energy system for the Al-Khalidiyah area. The primary objective is to provide a sustainable and reliable source of electricity and reduce reliance on fossil fuels. This study includes estimating the area's electricity demand, calculating the required solar system capacity, and assessing the environmental, social, and economic impacts. Additionally, the project explores how the use of renewable energy can contribute to improved sustainability and development within the area.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
127 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 379 0 R
/T ( DISTRICT DESCRIPTION)
/E ( DISTRICT DESCRIPTION)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
128 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 67 0 R
/E (Al-Khalidiyah is a well-developed residential area located in Kuwait City Governorate. The area is characterized by a blend of residential buildings, educational institutions, and local services, including educational institutions such as Abdullah Al-Salem University. The population of the area is estimated at approximately 19,000 to 20,000 based on available demographic data \(Central Statistical Bureau, n.d.\). As a developed urban area, Al-Khalidiyah consumes a significant amount of electricity, increasing the need for a reliable and, importantly, clean energy source. The primary reason for this increased electricity demand is primarily due to households, commercial activities, and the intensive use of air conditioning systems, especially given the extremely hot summer months \(International Energy Agency, n.d.\). Due to the increased energy demand in the area, the implementation of renewable energy systems, such as solar photovoltaic systems, is very suitable for the district.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
129 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 380 0 R
/T ( ENERGY  DEMAND  ESTIMATION)
/E ( ENERGY  DEMAND  ESTIMATION)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 381 0 R
/T ( RENEWABLE  ENERGY  SYSTEM  DESIGN)
/E ( RENEWABLE  ENERGY  SYSTEM  DESIGN)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (Khaldiya is a densely populated urban residential area in Kuwait, which is known for its high solar potential due to strong sunlight throughout the year. Implementing solar photovoltaic system deems perfect in order to harvest and harness solar energy that Kuwait yields. There are several ways to maximize efficiency such as adding Perovskites \(M. Mahmoud  2025\) . Firstly, the solar panel type has to be mono-crystalline  because it absorbs more energy compared to polycrystalline. Secondly, 30% of energy is lost due to sunlight being reflected off the solar panel, therefore, investing in anti-reflection coating with bifocals panels may increase cell efficiency. Thirdly, integrating ground-mounting solar arrays where trackers allow panels to follow sunlight may significantly increase solar efficiency. Finally, Khaldiya has twelve schools, fifteen mosques, cooperative societies and government office in addition to residential areas, covering their rooftops with solar panels should provide 88 MW worth of capacity. In addition to solar carports, 1.24 kilometers square is estimated whereas the required is 1.15 kilometers squared which is more than sufficient. According to NREL \(2023\) 128MW PV system should generate 218GWh annually, meeting 50% of the districts demands due to 5.5kWh/ meter square/ day of solar irradiance \(Al-Shammari & Al-Mahdi,2022\).)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (The annual electricity consumption for the Al-Khalidiyah area was estimated based on the population. Since there is no precise data on the area's electricity consumption rate, it was assumed that the average annual electricity consumption in Kuwait is 15,000 kWh/person/year. This reflects the high energy consumption in the country, due to the hot and harsh climate, which necessitates the intensive use of air conditioning systems \(Course Project Guidelines, 2025\). As previously mentioned, the population of the district is estimated at 19,300.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 68 0 R
/E (Based on these assumptions and information, the total annual electricity demand was calculated by multiplying the region's population by per capita consumption. Using this formula:)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
